      "text": "The given problem involves finding the number of different orders in which Jordan and his three best friends can run the relay team, given that Jordan is fixed to run the fourth lap. This means we only need to consider the permutations of the first three runners.\n\nLet's denote Jordan as \\( J \\) and his three friends as \\( F_1, F_2, \\) and \\( F_3 \\). The problem requires us to find the number of permutations of the sequence \\( F_1, F_2, F_3, J \\).\n\nTo find the number of permutations of these four runners, we use the formula for permutations of \\( n \\) distinct objects, which is given by \\( n! \\) (n factorial). For \\( n = 4 \\), we have:\n\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1\n\\]\n\nNow, let's calculate this step-by-step:\n\n\\[\n4 \\times 3 = 12\n\\]\n\\[\n12 \\times 2 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n24 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different orders in which the four team members can run, assuming that Jordan runs the fourth lap, is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{24}\n\\]",
